---
import Alignment from '../components/ExampleAlignment'
import Boundary from '../components/ExampleBoundary'
import IfNeeded from '../components/ExampleIfNeeded'
import OverrideBehavior from '../components/ExampleOverrideBehavior'
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro'
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro'
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
---

<Layout>
  <Hero />
  <main class="container is-fluid">
    <section class="intro columns">
      <div class="column">
        <h2 class="is-size-4">Ponyfill</h2>
        <p>
          This package <a href="http://ponyfill.com/">ponyfills</a>
          &nbsp;new features in the&nbsp;
          <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/scrollIntoView">
            Element.scrollIntoView
          </a>{' '}
          API. Including features in the non-standard{' '}
          <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/scrollIntoViewIfNeeded">
            Element.scrollIntoViewIfNeeded
          </a>{' '}
          API. The CSS working group has an open proposal{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/pull/5677">
            under discussion
          </a>{' '}
          for extending <code>scrollIntoView</code>. This library exposes{' '}
          <code>scrollMode: &quot;if-needed&quot;</code> as a library option; it is not an adopted standard.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="column">
        <h2 class="is-size-4">Minimal</h2>
        <p>
          The core package <code>scroll-into-view-if-needed</code> is
          small, using a shared geometry engine. That is why{' '}
          <code>behavior: &quot;smooth&quot;</code> is only ponyfilled if
          you use the add-on package{' '}
          <code>smooth-scroll-into-view-if-needed</code>. You don&#39;t need
          the add-on if native smooth scrolling is enough.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="column">
        <h2 class="is-size-4">Customizable</h2>
        <p>
          A couple of options not in the spec by the CSS working group helps
          customize scrolling to your needs. Avoid scrolling unwanted parent
          elements by using the <code>boundary: Element</code> option. Pass
          a function to <code>behavior</code> to customize exactly how
          elements are scrolled when <code>smooth</code> isn&#39;t enough.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section
      class="columns is-multiline is-desktop"
      id="scrolling-if-needed"
    >
      <div class="column is-one-third-desktop">
        <h3 class="title">Scrolling if needed</h3>
        <p class="subtitle">
          The library’s <code>scrollMode: 'if-needed'</code> option checks whether the target fits within the viewing region. The updated engine accounts for scroll margins and scroll padding at each ancestor.
        </p>
        <p>
          By default the browser controls the scrolling when{' '}
          <code>behavior: &#39;smooth&#39;</code> (unless you opt in to the
          ponyfill). Note there&#39;s browser differences with native smooth
          scrolling, like{' '}
          <a
            href="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/81981/38905887-9c00eff2-42b3-11e8-86aa-41ef679a54af.gif"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            target="_blank"
          >
            Chrome
          </a>{' '}
          vs{' '}
          <a
            href="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/81981/38905963-3065b790-42b4-11e8-9fab-35393d7b7d09.gif"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            target="_blank"
          >
            FireFox
          </a>{' '}
          in this{' '}
          <a href="https://codepen.io/stipsan/pen/NMxLew">CodePen</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="column is-two-thirds-desktop">
        <IfNeeded client:visible />
      </div>
    </section>
    <section
      class="columns is-multiline is-desktop"
      id="scroll-alignment"
    >
      <div class="column is-one-third-desktop">
        <h3 class="title">Scroll alignment</h3>
        <p class="subtitle">
          The position options for both <code>block</code> and{' '}
          <code>inline</code> are supported. Mix and match to your
          heart&#39;s content.
        </p>
        <p>
          Usually <code>block</code> aligns vertically, while{' '}
          <code>inline</code> aligns horizontally. It depends on the{' '}
          <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/writing-mode">
            writing-mode
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="column is-two-thirds-desktop">
        <Alignment client:visible />
      </div>
    </section>
    <section
      class="columns is-multiline is-desktop"
      id="limit-propagation"
    >
      <div class="column is-one-third-desktop">
        <h3 class="title">Limit propagation</h3>
        <p class="subtitle">
          Boundaries are good, that&#39;s what people keep saying. If you
          want some elements to scroll into view, but not all of the parents
          then <code>boundary</code> is the answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>boundary</code> is a library extension. CSSOM View now defines
          <code>container: 'nearest'</code> for stopping after the nearest scroll
          container, with <code>'all'</code> as the default. Native support varies;
          the accompanying engine update adds this option to the ponyfill.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="column is-two-thirds-desktop">
        <Boundary client:visible />
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="current-standards" aria-labelledby="standards-title">
      <h3 class="title" id="standards-title">Current scrolling standards</h3>
      <p class="subtitle">Standards and browser support checked 11 November 2026.</p>
      <div class="columns">
        <div class="column">
          <h4 class="is-size-5">Space around the target</h4>
          <p>
            Set <code>scroll-margin</code> on the target and <code>scroll-padding</code>
            on the scroll container to leave room for sticky headers or other content.
            These properties affect scrolling into view even without scroll snapping.
            The updated engine includes both, plus percentage padding and CSS math.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`.scroller { scroll-padding-top: 4rem; }
.target { scroll-margin-block: 1rem; }`}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="column">
          <h4 class="is-size-5">Wait for native scrolling</h4>
          <p>
            The <a href="https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom-view/#dom-element-scrollintoview">CSSOM View draft</a>{' '}
            specifies promises for <code>scroll()</code>, <code>scrollTo()</code>,
            <code>scrollBy()</code>, and <code>scrollIntoView()</code>. They resolve to
            <code>{'{ interrupted: boolean }'}</code>. The updated ponyfill combines
            native promises when available; older browsers still return undefined.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`const completion = scrollIntoView(target, {
  behavior: 'smooth',
})
if (completion) {
  const { interrupted } = await completion
}`}</code></pre>
          <p>Awaiting undefined does not wait for a legacy native smooth scroll.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="column">
          <h4 class="is-size-5">Keep focus in your control</h4>
          <p>
            <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#dom-focus">HTML defines focus()</a>{' '}
            separately. It returns undefined and accepts <code>preventScroll</code>.
            Proposed focus scroll options remain under discussion.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`target.focus({ preventScroll: true })
scrollIntoView(target, {
  scrollMode: 'if-needed',
  block: 'nearest',
})`}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The <a href="https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/pull/5677">if-needed proposal</a>{' '}
        remains open and currently uses a member named <code>scroll</code>.
        {' '}<code>scrollMode</code>, custom behavior callbacks, and <code>boundary</code>
        are library extensions. See the{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/scroll-into-view/compute-scroll-into-view/blob/main/docs/cssom-status.md">standards review</a>{' '}
        for compatibility, test coverage, and release order.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section
      class="columns is-multiline is-desktop"
      id="custom-transition"
    >
      <div class="column is-one-third-desktop">
        <h3 class="title">Custom transition: popmotion example</h3>
        <p class="subtitle">
          If you want a different easing, duration or another creative
          direction you can pass a function to <code>behavior</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Just like <code>boundary</code> this is not in the spec.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="column is-two-thirds-desktop">
        <OverrideBehavior client:visible />
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  #current-standards pre {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Links inside body copy need more than colour to stand out from the text */
  p a {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .intro {
    margin-left: auto !important;
    margin-right: auto !important;
    padding: 20px 0 !important;
    max-width: 1220px;
    margin-top: 1.5rem !important;

    padding-bottom: 3rem !important;
    margin-bottom: 3rem !important;
    position: relative;
  }

  .intro:after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 10px;
    right: 10px;
    bottom: 0;
    height: 2px;
    background: #f5f5f5;
  }
</style>
